From ed5735974a234f75e3dc5533278faa78e98a515c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 15:31:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 139/160] fix: the volumetric cloud layer was cut at the far
 plane - sky rays now march to the cloud shell

The hard horizontal line across the sky. Proven by experiment, not inference:
- pristine CRYENGINE 5.7.1 (engine DLLs and shaders from the official archive, stock Sandbox):
  same level, same camera, 18:00 - the line is there;
- last night's build (95930889 engine + stock editor + old plugin): the line is there;
- the same view with e_MaxViewDistance 20000 instead of the level's 8000: the line moves from
  ~6 deg to ~2.5 deg above the horizon, exactly asin((cloud base - camera) / ZF).

Root cause. Clouds.cfx limits the ray-march by the depth buffer ("limit the length of
ray-marching by depth buffer"). A sky pixel carries the far plane's depth, so the far plane
acts as an occluder: every ray whose slab entry lies beyond ZF gets maxStepNum == 0 and the
cloud layer - clouds, atmospheric haze, everything - stops at that elevation. Stock content
hides it under fog and a weak haze; the scene-referred path's brighter haze made it stark.

Fix. On the scene-referred path a sky ray (linear depth at the far plane) keeps the geometric
bounds - the cloud shell and the max viewable distance - and only real geometry clamps the
march. Both visible march techniques (monolithic and the split RenderCloud); the min-depth
twin follows. Gate = cloudBaseOffsetParams.w (stock: unused padding), filled 1.0 when the
scene-referred stage is on; off the switch the stock expressions are evaluated unchanged.
The overlay's cloud line prints "sky rays past ZF yes/no"; its zenith-haze number uses the
saturating 1 - exp(-beta OD) form.

Verified with the editor automation: at the level's own 8 km far plane the cloud layer now
runs continuously to the horizon at 17:30 and 18:00 (scene-notes/build-logs/sky_FINAL*.jpg
vs sky_OLD*, sky_PRISTINE2*_screen.png, sky_FAR20K*).

Not shipped, kept as an alternative: handing the empty-sky share of the Rayleigh haze to the
Nishita dome (research/s9-cloud-edge-root.md). It removes the haze's "sky layering", which the
user wants kept, and the line was never the haze's weight - it was the cut.

